0 0 1072693248 1073217536 -1073348608 | 0 1455541201 74834163 136167191 1534549342 | 849029948 -1195848122 1403326929 | -791501579 1458587647 -237204598 | 898984994 898984994 | -1390931727 -1390931727
--expect-why
COMPUTED FROM `clojure.lang.Murmur3` AND `clojure.lang.Util`, on Clojure 1.12
through the `clojure` CLI, in a script that implements the fold and the
combination independently of anything in this repository. Nothing below was
pasted out of a flint run.

    (import '[clojure.lang Murmur3 Util])
    (defn fold31 [bs]
      (reduce (fn [h b] (unchecked-int (unchecked-add (unchecked-multiply (int h) 31)
                                                      (bit-and b 0xFF))))
              (int 0) (seq bs)))
    (defn hb   [s]     (Murmur3/hashInt (fold31 (.getBytes s "UTF-8"))))
    (defn hsym [ns nm] (Util/hashCombine (hb nm) (hb (or ns ""))))
    (defn hkw  [ns nm] (unchecked-int (+ (hsym ns nm) (unchecked-int 0x9e3779b9))))
    (defn hd   [d]     (if (== d 0.0) 0
                         (let [b (Double/doubleToLongBits d)]
                           (unchecked-int (bit-xor b (unsigned-bit-shift-right b 32))))))

WHICH HALVES ARE CLOJURE'S ANSWER AND WHICH ARE FLINT'S, because the
distinction is real and the hand-written tests this replaces got it wrong.

  * THE DOUBLES ARE CLOJURE'S. `hd` is `Numbers.hasheq` for a double,
    transcribed: the two halves of the IEEE bit pattern xored, with zero
    special-cased. A flint program that hashed a double differently would
    disagree with Clojure, and that is a bug.

  * THE FIVE BYTE ROWS, THE SYMBOLS AND THE KEYWORDS ARE FLINT'S OWN, and
    `runtime/src/hash.rs` says so at length: text used to be hashed through
    `javaStringHashCode` and `hashUnencodedChars`, both of which walk UTF-16
    CODE UNITS, and reaching those numbers meant decoding UTF-8 on every
    lookup of a map key. Clojure changed its own hash in 1.6 and documents no
    stability across versions, so the numbers were never a contract.

    `RtHash.java` and `Program.cs` BOTH STILL SAY `clojure says` in the
    failure message for these rows. They have not been Clojure's numbers since
    the text hash moved to bytes; only the ASCII rows coincide, because there
    a byte IS a UTF-16 unit. `日本語` is the row that separates them and it is
    here for that reason.

    What the oracle above pins for those rows is the FORMULA, evaluated by
    somebody else's murmur3: `hash-int` of the 31-fold over the UTF-8 bytes.
    The fold is spelled out rather than borrowed from `String.hashCode`,
    because `String.hashCode` walks UTF-16 and would silently reintroduce the
    thing that was removed.

THE LAST PAIR IS THE PORT'S OWN NEW CLAIM, and it is why the pair is printed
rather than asserted. The three hand-written copies spelled "no namespace"
three ways -- `None` in Rust, `null` on both ports -- and this source has one
`Bytes` parameter where an ABSENT namespace is an EMPTY one. That is only
sound if

    hash-symbol [] name  ==  hash-combine (hash-bytes name) 0

`hash-bytes []` folds over nothing, giving 0, and `hash-int 0` short-circuits
to 0 -- so the two are the same expression. The driver evaluates BOTH SIDES
and prints them, and the oracle agrees: `(hsym nil "bar")` and `(hsym "" "bar")`
are both -1390931727. An equality asserted in the driver would have printed
`true` for two wrong numbers.

THE EMOJI PAIR IS PRINTED FOR THE SAME REASON. U+1F600 is one code point, four
UTF-8 bytes and two UTF-16 units; the hash must be over the four bytes. The
character and the literal `F0 9F 98 80` are hashed separately and both printed,
so a failure says which side moved instead of saying `false`.
